+#pragma once
+
+#include <string>
+#include <vector>
+
+// Player-installed content packs, read from a `mods` directory beside the
+// executable.
+//
+// The runner draws everything it shows -- text, music, cutscene video, sprite
+// sheets -- out of the cartridge image, so a player who wants to replace one of
+// them today patches the ROM. This module is the reading half of the
+// alternative: a directory of packs, each with a small manifest that maps a
+// game content id to a replacement file on disk. It only *describes* what
+// should be replaced; nothing here touches the emulator, and a pack that is
+// broken in any way is reported rather than enforced. A bad manifest must never
+// keep the game from starting.
+//
+// Two properties the rest of the runner depends on:
+//
+//   * Same directory contents, same result. Packs are visited in sorted name
+//     order, entries keep their manifest order, and both override tables come
+//     out sorted by key, so two runs over the same tree are comparable byte for
+//     byte, and the winner of a conflict does not depend on directory order.
+//   * Nothing is silent. Every problem becomes a diagnostic carrying the
+//     manifest path and, for a line-level problem, the line number. The worst
+//     outcome for a player is a mod that loads and quietly does nothing.
+//
+// The module owns no global state, allocates nothing process-wide, opens no
+// window and never prints: callers decide what a player sees, and the whole
+// thing is a pure function of the directory contents, so it is usable from a
+// test and safe to call from any thread.
+//
+// ## Manifest grammar
+//
+// `mods/<pack-name>/mod.toml` holds a deliberately tiny subset of TOML. It is
+// line-oriented and strict: anything outside the grammar below is an error with
+// a line number, never a guess. There is no TOML parser in this codebase and
+// this module does not add one, so the subset is small enough to state
+// completely.
+//
+//   * A line ends at `\n`; a `\r` before it is ignored. A leading UTF-8 byte
+//     order mark at the start of the file is ignored.
+//   * A comment starts at a `#` that is not inside a quoted string, and runs to
+//     the end of the line. A `#` inside a quoted string is ordinary text.
+//   * A blank or all-blank line is ignored. Leading and trailing blanks (space
+//     and tab) on any other line are ignored.
+//   * `[pack]` opens the pack table. It may appear at most once, at any
+//     position relative to the array tables, and holds string values only:
+//
+//         [pack]
+//         name = "Better Font"
+//         id = "better-font"        # a string, unlike a [[file]] id
+//         version = "1.2"           # optional
+//         description = "Readable text"   # optional
+//
+//   * `[[file]]` opens one file override, zero or more times. It holds a
+//     non-negative decimal integer content id and a string path relative to the
+//     pack directory:
+//
+//         [[file]]
+//         id = 4660                 # decimal only: no 0x, no sign
+//         path = "data/font.ntf"
+//
+//   * `[[music]]` opens one music override, zero or more times. It holds a
+//     non-negative decimal integer track number and a string path:
+//
+//         [[music]]
+//         track = 12
+//         path = "music/theme_12.bin"
+//
+//   * A key is `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*` and must be one of the fields above
+//     for the table it appears in. Repeating a key inside one table is an
+//     error; the second value is ignored so the first spelling stays stable.
+//   * A value is a quoted string or, for `id` and `track` only, a bare decimal
+//     integer. A quoted string accepts exactly the escapes `\"`, `\\`, `\n`,
+//     `\t` and `\r`; any other backslash sequence, a raw control byte, a
+//     missing closing quote or trailing text after the value is an error. Text
+//     outside those escapes is copied through as bytes, so UTF-8 names work
+//     without the manifest having to escape them.
+//
+// A field marked optional may be absent; every other field is required, so a
+// `[pack]` without `name` or `id`, and a `[[file]]`/`[[music]]` without its id
+// or `path`, rejects the pack with a diagnostic naming the missing field at the
+// line of its table header.
+//
+// A structural problem (syntax, unknown section or key, bad value, missing
+// field, self-contradiction) rejects the whole pack: half a pack applied to a
+// running game is worse than none, and the player has to edit the manifest
+// anyway. A bad *path* costs only its own entry -- the pack still applies the
+// entries that do resolve, because a pack with one stale file name is still
+// mostly useful.
+//
+// Two limits keep a hostile or accidentally huge manifest from delaying
+// startup: `mod.toml` may be at most 1 MiB and a line at most 4096 bytes.
+//
+// ## Paths
+//
+// A `path` must name a file inside the pack directory. In order, a path is
+// rejected when it is empty, starts with `/` or `\`, contains a `:` anywhere
+// (which covers a Windows drive letter, a UNC prefix and an NTFS alternate data
+// stream, on any host, so a pack authored on one platform cannot silently
+// address another), contains a `..` component, or resolves -- after following
+// symbolic links -- outside the pack directory. A path that passes those rules
+// but names a missing file or a directory is rejected too. Each rejection
+// reports the rule it broke, because "my mod does not work" is not a bug report
+// a player can act on.
+//
+// Every path the module reports is absolute-after-load, uses `/` as its
+// separator on every platform, has no `.` or empty components and no trailing
+// separator. That normalisation is what makes a test -- and a log line -- the
+// same on Windows and Linux; comparisons inside the module are made on native
+// path objects, never on these strings.
+
+namespace nds_mod_packs {
+
+// Why a diagnostic was produced. These are stable tokens, spelled by
+// nds_mod_pack_rule_name(), that a log line, a test or a settings page can
+// match on; the human sentence rides in the diagnostic's detail.
+enum class NdsModPackRule : int {
+    // Discovery.
+    MissingModsDirectory = 0,  // no `mods` directory: an ordinary install
+    DirectoryUnreadable,       // the `mods` directory could not be listed
+    DirectoryWithoutManifest,  // guidance only, see the model docs
+
+    // Manifest structure.
+    ManifestUnreadable,  // `mod.toml` exists but could not be read
+    ManifestTooLarge,    // over the 1 MiB manifest limit
+    ManifestSyntax,      // a line the grammar above does not accept
+    UnknownSection,      // `[something]` or `[[something]]` we do not know
+    DuplicateSection,    // `[pack]` given twice
+    UnknownKey,          // a key the current table does not define
+    DuplicateKey,        // the same key twice in one table
+    BadInteger,          // `id`/`track` is not a non-negative decimal
+    BadString,           // a string value with a bad escape or control byte
+    MissingField,        // a required field, or the `[pack]` table itself
+
+    // Referenced paths.
+    PathEmpty,             // `path = ""`
+    PathDriveLetter,       // any ':' in the path
+    PathLeadingSeparator,  // `\...` or `/...`, including a UNC share
+    PathParentTraversal,   // any `..` component
+    PathOutsidePack,       // resolves outside the pack directory
+    PathNotAFile,          // exists, but is not a regular file
+    PathMissing,           // does not exist, or could not be inspected
+
+    // Conflicts, inside one pack or between packs.
+    DuplicatePackId,     // two packs claim the same `[pack] id`
+    DuplicateFileId,     // one pack names the same content id twice
+    DuplicateMusicTrack, // one pack names the same track twice
+    ShadowedFile,        // a later pack's file id lost to an earlier pack
+    ShadowedMusic,       // a later pack's track lost to an earlier pack
+
+    // Volume.
+    TooManyProblems,  // diagnostics were dropped; the cap was reached
+};
+
+// Stable lowercase spelling of a rule ("path_parent_traversal"), for a log line
+// or a menu. Never returns nullptr; an out-of-range value yields "unknown".
+const char* nds_mod_pack_rule_name(NdsModPackRule rule);
+
+// A warning means something was skipped or is being shadowed and the game still
+// runs; an error means a pack, or one entry of it, was rejected.
+enum class NdsModPackSeverity : int {
+    Warning = 0,
+    Error,
+};
+
+const char* nds_mod_pack_severity_name(NdsModPackSeverity severity);
+
+// One problem found while loading. `file` is the manifest that produced it, or
+// the manifest path that was expected for DirectoryWithoutManifest, always with
+// forward slashes; it is empty for a problem about the `mods` directory itself.
+// `line` is 1-based, and 0 when the problem is not tied to a line. `pack` is
+// the pack directory name, and is empty for a discovery-level problem.
+struct NdsModPackDiagnostic {
+    NdsModPackSeverity severity = NdsModPackSeverity::Error;
+    NdsModPackRule rule = NdsModPackRule::ManifestSyntax;
+    std::string pack;
+    std::string file;
+    int line = 0;
+    std::string detail;
+};
+
+// One winning entry of an override table. `key` is the content id for a file
+// override and the track number for a music override. `path` is an absolute
+// host path naming an existing regular file.
+struct NdsModPackOverride {
+    int key = 0;
+    std::string pack;
+    std::string path;
+};
+
+// One pack as the launcher can show it. `directory` is the subdirectory name
+// under `mods` (what a player sees in their file manager); `enabled` is false
+// for a pack that was rejected, in which case `name` and `id` are empty because
+// the manifest never produced a usable table.
+struct NdsModPackInfo {
+    std::string directory;
+    std::string name;
+    std::string id;
+    std::string version;
+    std::string description;
+    bool enabled = false;
+};
+
+// Counts for one line of UI. `packs` is every immediate subdirectory of the
+// `mods` directory, with or without a manifest, and `enabled_packs` is how many
+// of those produced a usable table. A missing `mods` directory leaves both at
+// zero and is the only diagnostic that is completely ordinary.
+struct NdsModPacksSummary {
+    int packs = 0;
+    int enabled_packs = 0;
+    int file_overrides = 0;
+    int music_overrides = 0;
+    int warnings = 0;
+    int errors = 0;
+};
+
+// The load result: what the game may use, and everything that went wrong.
+// Returned by value rather than through a bool and an out-parameter, so a
+// caller always has a table to consult -- an empty one when every pack was
+// rejected, which is exactly what the runner needs to keep starting.
+struct NdsModPacksResult {
+    std::vector<NdsModPackOverride> files;  // sorted by key, ascending
+    std::vector<NdsModPackOverride> music;  // sorted by key, ascending
+    std::vector<NdsModPackInfo> packs;      // sorted by directory name
+    std::vector<NdsModPackDiagnostic> diagnostics;
+    NdsModPacksSummary summary;
+};
+
+// Reads every pack under `mods_directory`. Never throws and never fails as a
+// whole: a missing directory, an unreadable manifest or a rejected pack all
+// come back as diagnostics alongside whatever table survived. Plain files
+// directly under `mods_directory` are not packs and are ignored -- only an
+// immediate subdirectory without a `mod.toml` is reported, because that is what
+// a player creates by mistake when they unzip a pack one level too deep.
+//
+// When two packs claim the same content id or track, the pack whose directory
+// name sorts first wins and every loser is reported as shadowed, so a player
+// can see which two packs are fighting over one file.
+NdsModPacksResult nds_mod_packs_load(const std::string& mods_directory);
+
+// "<directory of `executable_path`>/mods". Pure string work, so it does not
+// care whether the path or that directory exists and behaves the same on every
+// platform; an empty input, or one with no directory part, yields "mods".
+std::string nds_mod_packs_default_directory(const std::string& executable_path);
+
+// Path the winning override for `content_id` (a file) or `track` (music) names,
+// or nullptr when no pack supplies it. The pointer stays valid while `result`
+// does, and points at an empty string never.
+const std::string* nds_mod_packs_file_path(const NdsModPacksResult& result,
+                                           int content_id);
+const std::string* nds_mod_packs_music_path(const NdsModPacksResult& result,
+                                            int track);
+
+// Validates one pack-relative path against the rules in "## Paths" above,
+// without a loaded pack: `pack_directory` is the pack root, `relative` the
+// value from a manifest. On success writes the normalised absolute path to
+// `resolved` and returns true. On failure returns false and, when the pointers
+// are non-null, sets `*rule` to the rule that was broken and `*detail` to a
+// sentence naming the offending path. Exposed so a tool can explain a manifest
+// without starting the emulator.
+bool nds_mod_pack_resolve_path(const std::string& pack_directory,
+                               const std::string& relative,
+                               std::string* resolved, NdsModPackRule* rule,
+                               std::string* detail);
+
+// One diagnostic as a single line, for a log file or a launcher pane:
+//
+//     error path_missing: no such file: 'a/b.bin' (mods/My Pack/mod.toml:7)
+//
+// The parenthesised location is left off when the diagnostic has no file, and
+// the ":<line>" when it has no line. Returns a string instead of printing so
+// the caller decides whether a player ever sees it.
+std::string nds_mod_pack_diagnostic_text(
+    const NdsModPackDiagnostic& diagnostic);
+
+}  // namespace nds_mod_packs
